.IX Title "Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer 3pm"
.TH Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer \- Split a string into tokens.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\&    my $whitespace_tokenizer
\&        = Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer\->new( pattern => \*(Aq\eS+\*(Aq );
\&
\&    # or...
\&    my $word_char_tokenizer
\&        = Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer\->new( pattern => \*(Aq\ew+\*(Aq );
\&
\&    # or...
\&    my $apostrophising_tokenizer = Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer\->new;
\&
\&    # Then... once you have a tokenizer, put it into a PolyAnalyzer:
\&    my $polyanalyzer = Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer\->new(
\&        analyzers => [ $word_char_tokenizer, $normalizer, $stemmer ], );
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
Generically, “tokenizing” is a process of breaking up a string into an
array of “tokens”.  For instance, the string “three blind mice” might be
tokenized into “three”, “blind”, “mice”.
.PP
Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer decides where it should break up the text
based on a regular expression compiled from a supplied \f(CW\*(C`pattern\*(C'\fR
matching one token.  If our source string is…
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    "Eats, Shoots and Leaves."
.Ve
.PP
… then a “whitespace tokenizer” with a \f(CW\*(C`pattern\*(C'\fR of
\&\f(CW"\e\eS+"\fR produces…
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    Eats,
\&    Shoots
\&    and
\&    Leaves.
.Ve
.PP
… while a “word character tokenizer” with a \f(CW\*(C`pattern\*(C'\fR of
\&\f(CW"\e\ew+"\fR produces…
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    Eats
\&    Shoots
\&    and
\&    Leaves
.Ve
.PP
… the difference being that the word character tokenizer skips over
punctuation as well as whitespace when determining token boundaries.
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.IX Header "CONSTRUCTORS"
.SS new
.IX Subsection "new"
.Vb 3
\&    my $word_char_tokenizer = Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer\->new(
\&        pattern => \*(Aq\ew+\*(Aq,    # required
\&    );
.Ve
.PP
Create a new RegexTokenizer.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBpattern\fR \- A string specifying a Perl-syntax regular expression
which should match one token.  The default value is
\&\f(CW\*(C`\ew+(?:[\ex{2019}\*(Aq]\ew+)*\*(C'\fR, which matches “it’s” as well as
“it” and “O’Henry’s” as well as “Henry”.
.SH METHODS
.IX Header "METHODS"
.SS transform
.IX Subsection "transform"
.Vb 1
\&    my $inversion = $regex_tokenizer\->transform($inversion);
.Ve
.PP
Take a single Inversion as input
and returns an Inversion, either the same one (presumably transformed
in some way), or a new one.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBinversion\fR \- An inversion.
.SH INHERITANCE
.IX Header "INHERITANCE"
Lucy::Analysis::RegexTokenizer isa Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer isa Clownfish::Obj.
